    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Disaster_Response_Logistics\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)  # Set time limit to 300 seconds\n\n    # Data\n    depots = list(range(2))  # D0..D1\n    units = list(range(4))  # U0..U3\n    supplies = list(range(3))  # S0..S2\n    days = list(range(1))  # Day0..Day0\n\n    demands = [[[34.08], [30.94], [42.08]], [[25.98], [30.77], [40.21]], [[22.56], [23.11], [46.97]], [[41.81], [39.33], [30.68]]]\n    depot_supplies = [[[73.08], [87.27], [109.37]], [[78.64], [79.93], [119.69]]]\n    transport_costs = [[1.14, 1.33, 3.82, 3.95], [1.81, 2.62, 4.0, 4.15]]\n    risk_costs = [[199.68, 62.02, 54.83, 123.46], [161.51, 172.87, 78.9, 54.02]]\n    supply_volumes = [0.79, 0.68, 1.62]\n    penalty_costs = [72.04, 75.37, 90.7]\n    vehicle_cost = 10.0\n    vehicle_capacity = 100.0\n    total_vehicles = 20\n\n    # Decision variables\n    # Flow_D{d}_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}: Amount of supply s from depot d to unit u on day t\n    flows = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                for s in supplies:\n                    flows[(d, u, s, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_D{d}_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Slack_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}: Shortage of supply s at unit u on day t\n    slacks = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for u in units:\n            for s in supplies:\n                slacks[(u, s, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Slack_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Veh_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}: Number of vehicles on route d->u on day t\n    vehicles = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                vehicles[(d, u, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.INTEGER, name=f\"Veh_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Route_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}: Route d->u is active on day t (binary)\n    routes = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                routes[(d, u, t)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Route_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize total cost (transport + penalty + vehicle + risk)\n    obj = quicksum(\n        transport_costs[d][u] * flows[(d, u, s, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units for s in supplies\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        penalty_costs[s] * slacks[(u, s, t)]\n        for t in days for u in units for s in supplies\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        vehicle_cost * vehicles[(d, u, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        risk_costs[d][u] * routes[(d, u, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Depot capacity constraints: Total outflow <= supply\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for s in supplies:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(flows[(d, u, s, t)] for u in units) <= depot_supplies[d][s][t],\n                    name=f\"DepotCap_D{d}_S{s}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Demand constraints: Inflow + slack >= demand\n    for t in days:\n        for u in units:\n            for s in supplies:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(flows[(d, u, s, t)] for d in depots) + slacks[(u, s, t)] >= demands[u][s][t],\n                    name=f\"Demand_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Vehicle capacity constraints: Total volume <= vehicles * capacity\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(supply_volumes[s] * flows[(d, u, s, t)] for s in supplies) <= vehicle_capacity * vehicles[(d, u, t)],\n                    name=f\"VehCap_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Route activation constraints: vehicles <= total_vehicles * route\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    vehicles[(d, u, t)] <= total_vehicles * routes[(d, u, t)],\n                    name=f\"RouteAct_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Fleet size constraints: Total vehicles <= total_vehicles\n    for t in days:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(vehicles[(d, u, t)] for d in depots for u in units) <= total_vehicles,\n            name=f\"FleetSize_Day{t}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Situation:\nA severe storm has disrupted road networks and displaced communities across the operational area. Supply Depot Alpha (coastal logistics hub) and Supply Depot Bravo (inland warehouse) hold mixed stocks of medical aid, safe water supplies, and shelter materials. Forward Operating Bases Delta, Echo, Foxtrot, and Golf are receiving points for affected districts, coordinating onward distribution to shelters and clinics. Several ground corridors are passable but degraded by flooding, landslides, and intermittent security incidents. Movement is constrained to daylight hours, with variable road clearance status and weight restrictions on temporary bridges. Airlift is limited; ground convoys are the primary mode.\n\nMission:\nDeliver essential supplies from depots to forward units during the current operational period to stabilize critical needs while minimizing total operational cost, comprising:\n- Transport costs per unit shipped\n- Shortage penalty costs for unmet demand\n- Vehicle deployment costs\n- Fixed route security risk costs for each activated corridor\n\nExecution/Concept:\n- Approach: Consolidate loads at depots and push to forward units over the safest feasible corridors. Prioritize life-sustaining commodities, concentrate shipments on lower-risk routes, and avoid opening high-risk corridors for marginal loads. Where risk or access precludes full delivery, allow controlled shortfalls with penalty.\n- Depot capacity: Outbound flows of each supply type from each depot cannot exceed available stocks by location and commodity.\n- Demand coverage: Inbound flows to each forward unit by commodity should meet stated demand; any residual gap is modeled as slack and incurs a penalty that reflects humanitarian impact and operational risk tolerance.\n- Vehicle capacity: On each depot-to-unit corridor, the total loaded volume of all commodities cannot exceed the product of assigned vehicles and vehicle capacity. Per-vehicle deployment costs apply.\n- Route activation and security overhead: A corridor is considered active once any vehicle is dispatched on it. Activating a route triggers a fixed Risk Cost that reflects escorts, route clearance, and command-and-control overhead, regardless of how many vehicles use that route during the period.\n- Fleet cap: The total number of vehicles assigned across all corridors cannot exceed fleet availability for the period.\n- Cost drivers: Per-route, per-commodity transport costs guide routing decisions. Fixed route risk costs, vehicle costs, and shortage penalties are balanced to achieve minimum total cost.\n- Coordination: Movement Control will synchronize convoy timings with engineering and security elements for route clearance and escort. Forward units will stage offload teams to reduce dwell time. Contingency plans include consolidating shipments onto fewer corridors if weather or security deteriorates.\n\nAnnex A - Logistics Data:\nUnit Demands (Units x Supplies):\nUnit\\Supply | S 0 | S 1 | S 2\n-----------------------------\nU 0        |  34.08 |  30.94 |  42.08\nU 1        |  25.98 |  30.77 |  40.21\nU 2        |  22.56 |  23.11 |  46.97\nU 3        |  41.81 |  39.33 |  30.68\nDepot Supplies (Depots x Supplies):\nDepot\\Supply | S 0 | S 1 | S 2\n------------------------------\nD 0        |  73.08 |  87.27 | 109.37\nD 1        |  78.64 |  79.93 | 119.69\nTransport Costs (Depot x Unit):\nDepot\\Unit | U 0 | U 1 | U 2 | U 3\n----------------------------------\nD 0        |   1.14 |   1.33 |   3.82 |   3.95\nD 1        |   1.81 |   2.62 |   4.00 |   4.15\nRisk Costs (Depot x Unit):\nDepot\\Unit | U 0 | U 1 | U 2 | U 3\n----------------------------------\nD 0        | 199.68 |  62.02 |  54.83 | 123.46\nD 1        | 161.51 | 172.87 |  78.90 |  54.02\nPenalty Costs per Unit Shortage: [Supply S0: 72.04, Supply S1: 75.37, Supply S2: 90.7]\nVolume per Supply Unit: [Supply S0: 0.79, Supply S1: 0.68, Supply S2: 1.62]\nFleet Parameters: Cost 10.0, Capacity 100.0, Total 20",
    "LLM_description_template": "Situation:\nA severe storm has disrupted road networks and displaced communities across the operational area. Supply Depot Alpha (coastal logistics hub) and Supply Depot Bravo (inland warehouse) hold mixed stocks of medical aid, safe water supplies, and shelter materials. Forward Operating Bases Delta, Echo, Foxtrot, and Golf are receiving points for affected districts, coordinating onward distribution to shelters and clinics. Several ground corridors are passable but degraded by flooding, landslides, and intermittent security incidents. Movement is constrained to daylight hours, with variable road clearance status and weight restrictions on temporary bridges. Airlift is limited; ground convoys are the primary mode.\n\nMission:\nDeliver essential supplies from depots to forward units during the current operational period to stabilize critical needs while minimizing total operational cost, comprising:\n- Transport costs per unit shipped\n- Shortage penalty costs for unmet demand\n- Vehicle deployment costs\n- Fixed route security risk costs for each activated corridor\n\nExecution/Concept:\n- Approach: Consolidate loads at depots and push to forward units over the safest feasible corridors. Prioritize life-sustaining commodities, concentrate shipments on lower-risk routes, and avoid opening high-risk corridors for marginal loads. Where risk or access precludes full delivery, allow controlled shortfalls with penalty.\n- Depot capacity: Outbound flows of each supply type from each depot cannot exceed available stocks by location and commodity.\n- Demand coverage: Inbound flows to each forward unit by commodity should meet stated demand; any residual gap is modeled as slack and incurs a penalty that reflects humanitarian impact and operational risk tolerance.\n- Vehicle capacity: On each depot-to-unit corridor, the total loaded volume of all commodities cannot exceed the product of assigned vehicles and vehicle capacity. Per-vehicle deployment costs apply.\n- Route activation and security overhead: A corridor is considered active once any vehicle is dispatched on it. Activating a route triggers a fixed Risk Cost that reflects escorts, route clearance, and command-and-control overhead, regardless of how many vehicles use that route during the period.\n- Fleet cap: The total number of vehicles assigned across all corridors cannot exceed fleet availability for the period.\n- Cost drivers: Per-route, per-commodity transport costs guide routing decisions. Fixed route risk costs, vehicle costs, and shortage penalties are balanced to achieve minimum total cost.\n- Coordination: Movement Control will synchronize convoy timings with engineering and security elements for route clearance and escort. Forward units will stage offload teams to reduce dwell time. Contingency plans include consolidating shipments onto fewer corridors if weather or security deteriorates.\n\nAnnex A - Logistics Data:\n{DEMANDS}\n{DEPOT_SUPPLIES}\n{TRANSPORT_COSTS}\n{RISK_COSTS}\n{PENALTY_COSTS}\n{SUPPLY_VOLUMES}\nFleet Parameters: Cost {VEHICLE_COST}, Capacity {VEHICLE_CAPACITY}, Total {TOTAL_VEHICLES}"
